  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why \"pure\" gold is labeled as 99.9% (or 999 fineness) rather than 100%:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udd2c True 100% Purity is Physically Impossible\nBoth 24-karat and 999.9 fineness are generally accepted as pure gold. Despite this, in truth it is **impossible to produce 100% pure gold** based on current technical limits.\n\nPerfect purity would mean a piece of gold has zero non-gold atoms. But nature has its own plans \u2014 pure gold naturally has some other atoms mingling in there. Plus, these so-called \"impurities\" actually have a job: they help stabilize the metal's structure.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \u2697\ufe0f The Refining Process Has Natural Limits\nRefining metals to remove impurities costs money. There's no benefit in making gold bullion used for monetary or jewelry purposes any better than 99.99%, as doing so just raises costs with no real benefit.\n\nIt is currently impossible to refine gold to a purity of 100% (1,000 fineness). 999.999 fineness is the purest gold ever produced, and proved too expensive to be a regular occurrence.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udccf How Purity is Measured (The Millesimal Fineness Scale)\nGold's purity is graded based on the **millesimal fineness scale**, which determines the purity of a precious metal by measuring the number of parts per thousand of pure metal. Typically, anything that is 99.9% gold (999) or more is considered **fine gold**.\n\nThe purest gold commercially available is 999.99, also called **\"five nines\" fine**. Just below this is 999.9 gold, also called **\"four nines\" fine**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83c\udfe6 Industry Standards & Practical Use\nGenerally, the closest to 100% pure gold that refiners are able to reach in everyday production is a **fineness of 999.9 gold**.\n\nThe British Royal Mint, the Royal Canadian Mint, and the Perth Mint all strike their gold coins to a purity of 999.9 gold, making it one of the most commonly seen purities for bullion coins and bars.\n\n---\n\n### In Summary\nGold is labeled as 99.9% (or higher) rather than 100% because achieving absolute atomic purity is **physically and technically impossible** with current technology. Trace amounts of other elements always remain, and some even serve a purpose in stabilizing gold's structure. The 99.9% standard is simply the practical benchmark the industry has set for what is commercially considered \"pure\" gold.",
